Method:

```
static void checkDiscriminatorMatch(final DiscriminatorContext currentDiscriminatorContext, final ObjectNode discriminator, final String discriminatorPropertyValue, final JsonSchema jsonSchema) {
    if (discriminatorPropertyValue == null) {
        currentDiscriminatorContext.markIgnore();
        return;
    }
    final JsonNode discriminatorMapping = discriminator.get("mapping");
    if (null == discriminatorMapping) {
        checkForImplicitDiscriminatorMappingMatch(currentDiscriminatorContext, discriminatorPropertyValue, jsonSchema);
    } else {
        checkForExplicitDiscriminatorMappingMatch(currentDiscriminatorContext, discriminatorPropertyValue, discriminatorMapping, jsonSchema);
        if ((!currentDiscriminatorContext.isDiscriminatorMatchFound()) && noExplicitDiscriminatorKeyOverride(discriminatorMapping, jsonSchema)) {
            checkForImplicitDiscriminatorMappingMatch(currentDiscriminatorContext, discriminatorPropertyValue, jsonSchema);
        }
    }
}
```

Potential exceptions:

- discriminatorPropertyValue null handled, no exception.
- discriminator null leads to NPE at discriminator.get("mapping") (already covered).
- currentDiscriminatorContext null leads to NPE when calling markIgnore or later methods. Not covered.
- jsonSchema null leads to passing null to checkForImplicit/Explicit methods; those methods may NPE internally, but from this method perspective, no immediate NPE. However calling checkForImplicitDiscriminatorMappingMatch with jsonSchema null could cause NPE inside that method; but we can't guarantee. Still we can consider that method may not accept null and could throw NullPointerException. Since we are focusing on this method, the only direct dereference of jsonSchema is passing to other methods; no NPE here. However if jsonSchema is null and discriminatorPropertyValue null? The early return avoids using jsonSchema. So no exception.

- discriminator.get("mapping") may throw if discriminator is not an ObjectNode? But parameter type is ObjectNode, compile-time ensures. However could be a subclass that overrides get to throw? Unlikely.

- The call to noExplicitDiscriminatorKeyOverride(discriminatorMapping, jsonSchema) may throw NullPointerException if discriminatorMapping is null, but it's inside else where discriminatorMapping not null.

- If discriminator.get("mapping") returns a JsonNode that is not an object, still fine.

Thus exception scenarios: null currentDiscriminatorContext, null discriminator (covered), maybe discriminator.get throws JsonProcessingException? Not.

Also if currentDiscriminatorContext methods (markIgnore, isDiscriminatorMatchFound) throw runtime exceptions (e.g., IllegalStateException) due to misuse; but not part of method.

